"""Calendar, date and time pickers, and the field a date is typed into.

The pieces of the design's calendar.html that need the runtime: focus that
moves with the arrow keys, a month that changes without re-rendering the
page, and names and digits from the browser's ``Intl``. Every value they
take or hand back is an ISO string (``"2025-12-16"``), the contract
``workpeg.ui.dates`` computes in, so a picked day goes straight into
``dates.add_days`` or a ``Field`` with no conversion.

The compiler reads these classes' keywords from its own table
(``_CALENDAR_KEYWORDS`` in ``compiler/ast_parser.py``) and the runtime from
``CAL_OPTIONS``; a test pins the three together, so a keyword cannot exist
here and be unknown to either. ``CalendarCard``, the card a composed picker
sits in, has no date to read: its two keywords are checked by an arm of
its own (``_CALENDAR_CARD_KEYWORDS``), pinned to its signature the same way.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

from typing import Any, Callable, Literal

from workpeg.ui.state import Binding
from workpeg.ui.widgets.base import BaseWidget, WidgetChild


#: ``header=``: the title on the left and the arrows on the right, the
#: title between the arrows, or no header at all.
CALENDAR_HEADERS = ("default", "compact", "none")

#: ``surface=``: the design's card, or flat for a calendar inside a panel
#: that draws its own (``.calendar--embedded``).
CALENDAR_SURFACES = ("card", "flat")

#: ``initial_calendar_mode=``: what a calendar shows first, its days or its
#: years.
CALENDAR_MODES = ("day", "year")

#: ``months=``: how many months a calendar shows side by side, the design's
#: range of flat months under one pair of arrows when more than one.
CALENDAR_MONTHS = (1, 2, 3)

#: ``minute_interval=``: the steps a time wheel's minutes may take, every
#: whole number that divides an hour, so the column ends one step before
#: the next hour and every hour shows the same minutes.
TIME_PICKER_INTERVALS = (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 10, 12, 15, 20, 30)


class CalendarDatePicker(BaseWidget):
    """A month of days to pick from — Flutter's ``CalendarDatePicker``,
    drawn as the design's calendar card.

    ``value`` is the picked day, ``"2025-12-16"``, or ``None``; ``on_change``
    receives the day pressed. With ``multiple=True`` the value is a list,
    kept sorted and without repeats, and a press adds or removes that day::

        CalendarDatePicker(value=self.day, on_change=self.set_day)
        CalendarDatePicker(value=self.days, on_change=self.set_days,
                           multiple=True)

    Pressing the picked day again does nothing, as in Flutter, which has no
    deselect. The calendar is controlled: the day shown as picked is always
    ``value``, so a handler that ignores a press leaves the page as it was.

    **Which days can be picked.** ``first_date`` and ``last_date`` bound it
    (1900-01-01 to 2100-12-31 by default; one given alone beyond the other's
    default leaves that side open, so ``last_date="1899-12-31"`` reaches
    back to 0001-01-01); ``disabled_weekdays=[6, 7]``
    rules out weekends, ``disabled_dates`` a list of days, and
    ``selectable_day_predicate`` anything else (``lambda d: …``, a method or
    a callable prop, called with the ISO date). A day must pass all four. A
    disabled day can still be focused, so a screen reader can say it is
    unavailable, but pressing it does nothing.

    **Markers.** ``markers`` is a list of days with one dot each, or a dict
    of day to a count; ``event_loader`` is a function from a day to a list,
    whose length is the count. Dots are capped at ``markers_max_count``
    (1 to 4, default 1).

    **The month shown.** The value's month, or today's (``current_date``, by
    default today in ``time_zone``), moved by the arrows and kept across the
    re-renders a state write causes. When ``value`` changes to a day in a
    month that is not shown, the calendar moves to it.
    ``displayed_month="2025-12"`` controls it instead, and
    ``on_displayed_month_changed`` receives ``"YYYY-MM"`` whenever the
    person moves it.

    **The year view.** The default header's title is a button: it swaps
    the days for a grid of every year in the bounds, and a year pressed
    there goes back to the days on the same month of that year (Feb 29
    lands on Feb 28). ``initial_calendar_mode="year"`` opens on the years.
    Neither changes ``value``, and neither re-renders the page.

    **Several months.** ``months=2`` or ``3`` draws that many side by side,
    flat, each under a static title, with one Previous and one Next for
    all of them, as the design's range does; on a narrow screen only the
    first shows. There is no year view and no compact header then, and a
    neighbouring month's day is blank unless ``outside_days_visible=True``.

    **Custom days.** ``day_builder=lambda day: <widget>`` draws each day's
    content in place of its number, from a dict of the day (``date``,
    ``label``, ``day``, ``weekday``, ``outside``, ``today``, ``selected``,
    ``in_range``, ``range_start``, ``range_end``, ``disabled``, ``events``,
    ``markers``); a widget helper of one parameter, ``self.day_cell``, is
    the same. The day is still the button, so the content may only be
    static widgets (``Text``, ``Icon``, ``Dot``, a ``Tag``, ``Image`` and
    the boxes that arrange them), never a control, and no handler. What
    it draws takes the day's colour, white on the day picked; a ``Text``
    with ``variant="caption"`` or ``"muted"``, or a ``tone``, keeps its
    own, so the count below is made small with ``style=`` instead::

        CalendarDatePicker(event_loader=self.events_on,
                           day_builder=lambda day: Column(
                               Text(day["label"]),
                               Text(str(len(day["events"])),
                                    style={"font-size": "10px",
                                           "line-height": "12px"})))

    **Looks.** ``header`` is ``"default"``, ``"compact"`` (the title between
    the arrows) or ``"none"``; ``surface="flat"`` drops the card;
    ``outside_days_visible=False`` blanks the neighbouring months' days;
    ``six_week_months_enforced=True`` always draws six weeks. ``day_style``
    is a ``style=`` dict for every day, state keys included (``":hover"``,
    ``":selected"``, ``":current"`` for today, ``":disabled"``),
    ``year_style`` the same for every year of the year view, and ``style=``
    takes the ``--calendar-*`` tokens.

    **Language.** Month and weekday names, digits and the first day of the
    week come from ``locale`` (default: the page's language), unless
    ``starting_day_of_week`` (1 Monday … 7 Sunday, ``dates.MONDAY`` …)
    sets it. ``label`` names the calendar for a screen reader.
    """

    widget_type = "CalendarDatePicker"

    def __init__(
        self,
        *,
        value: str | list | Binding | None = None,
        on_change: Callable | None = None,
        multiple: bool = False,
        first_date: str | Binding | None = None,
        last_date: str | Binding | None = None,
        current_date: str | Binding | None = None,
        displayed_month: str | Binding | None = None,
        on_displayed_month_changed: Callable | None = None,
        initial_calendar_mode: Literal["day", "year"] | Binding = "day",
        header: Literal["default", "compact", "none"] | Binding = "default",
        months: int | Binding = 1,
        surface: Literal["card", "flat"] | Binding = "card",
        outside_days_visible: bool | Binding | None = None,
        six_week_months_enforced: bool | Binding = False,
        starting_day_of_week: int | Binding | None = None,
        selectable_day_predicate: Callable | None = None,
        disabled_dates: list | Binding | None = None,
        disabled_weekdays: list | Binding | None = None,
        markers: list | dict | Binding | None = None,
        event_loader: Callable | None = None,
        markers_max_count: int | Binding = 1,
        day_builder: Callable | None = None,
        day_style: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        year_style: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        locale: str | Binding | None = None,
        time_zone: str | Binding | None = None,
        label: str | Binding | None = None,
        disabled: bool | Binding = False,
        style: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        key: str | None = None,
        **kwargs: Any,
    ):
        super().__init__(key=key, disabled=disabled, **kwargs)
        self.value = value
        self.on_change = on_change
        self.multiple = multiple
        self.first_date = first_date
        self.last_date = last_date
        self.current_date = current_date
        self.displayed_month = displayed_month
        self.on_displayed_month_changed = on_displayed_month_changed
        self.initial_calendar_mode = initial_calendar_mode
        self.header = header
        self.months = months
        self.surface = surface
        self.outside_days_visible = outside_days_visible
        self.six_week_months_enforced = six_week_months_enforced
        self.starting_day_of_week = starting_day_of_week
        self.selectable_day_predicate = selectable_day_predicate
        self.disabled_dates = disabled_dates
        self.disabled_weekdays = disabled_weekdays
        self.markers = markers
        self.event_loader = event_loader
        self.markers_max_count = markers_max_count
        self.day_builder = day_builder
        self.day_style = day_style
        self.year_style = year_style
        self.locale = locale
        self.time_zone = time_zone
        self.label = label
        self.style = style

    #: Each keyword's default, so ``props()`` reports only what was set.
    _DEFAULTS = {"multiple": False, "initial_calendar_mode": "day",
                 "header": "default", "months": 1, "surface": "card",
                 "six_week_months_enforced": False, "markers_max_count": 1}

    #: The keywords ``props()`` reports, when set and not the default.
    _PROPS = ("value", "multiple", "first_date", "last_date",
              "current_date", "displayed_month", "initial_calendar_mode",
              "header", "months", "surface", "outside_days_visible",
              "six_week_months_enforced", "starting_day_of_week",
              "disabled_dates", "disabled_weekdays", "markers",
              "markers_max_count", "day_style", "year_style", "locale",
              "time_zone", "label", "style")

    def props(self) -> dict[str, Any]:
        out: dict[str, Any] = {}
        for name in self._PROPS:
            value = getattr(self, name)
            if value is not None and value != self._DEFAULTS.get(name):
                out[name] = value
        return out


class CalendarDateRangePicker(BaseWidget):
    """A range of days to pick, across up to three months — Flutter's
    ``CalendarDateRangePicker``, drawn as the design's range (calendar.html,
    Multi-month).

    ``value`` is ``{"start": "2025-12-16", "end": "2025-12-24"}``, or
    ``None``; ``on_change`` receives the new dict. The first press starts a
    range, ``{"start": d, "end": None}`` (the open end is always there, as
    ``None``), the next ends it on that day or a later one, and a press
    before the start starts again from there::

        CalendarDateRangePicker(value=self.stay, on_change=self.set_stay,
                                months=2)

    A band joins the two ends across rows and months, and while a range is
    open the days up to the one hovered or focused are previewed, without
    re-rendering the page. ``range_length=7`` makes every press the whole
    span, the day and the six after it, and disables a day whose span would
    pass ``last_date``. Days that cannot be picked may sit inside a range,
    as in Flutter.

    It takes every keyword of ``CalendarDatePicker`` but ``multiple``, and
    ``selectable_day_predicate`` may take three arguments, ``(day, start,
    end)``, the range picked so far (each ``None`` when absent). A
    ``day_builder``'s dict says where a day sits in the range
    (``in_range``, ``range_start``, ``range_end``). Inside a
    ``Field`` the validators read ``"start/end"``, or ``""`` while only the
    start is picked, so ``required`` refuses an unfinished range;
    ``on_submit`` receives the dict.
    """

    widget_type = "CalendarDateRangePicker"

    def __init__(
        self,
        *,
        value: dict | Binding | None = None,
        on_change: Callable | None = None,
        range_length: int | Binding | None = None,
        first_date: str | Binding | None = None,
        last_date: str | Binding | None = None,
        current_date: str | Binding | None = None,
        displayed_month: str | Binding | None = None,
        on_displayed_month_changed: Callable | None = None,
        initial_calendar_mode: Literal["day", "year"] | Binding = "day",
        header: Literal["default", "compact", "none"] | Binding = "default",
        months: int | Binding = 1,
        surface: Literal["card", "flat"] | Binding = "card",
        outside_days_visible: bool | Binding | None = None,
        six_week_months_enforced: bool | Binding = False,
        starting_day_of_week: int | Binding | None = None,
        selectable_day_predicate: Callable | None = None,
        disabled_dates: list | Binding | None = None,
        disabled_weekdays: list | Binding | None = None,
        markers: list | dict | Binding | None = None,
        event_loader: Callable | None = None,
        markers_max_count: int | Binding = 1,
        day_builder: Callable | None = None,
        day_style: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        year_style: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        locale: str | Binding | None = None,
        time_zone: str | Binding | None = None,
        label: str | Binding | None = None,
        disabled: bool | Binding = False,
        style: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        key: str | None = None,
        **kwargs: Any,
    ):
        super().__init__(key=key, disabled=disabled, **kwargs)
        self.value = value
        self.on_change = on_change
        self.range_length = range_length
        self.first_date = first_date
        self.last_date = last_date
        self.current_date = current_date
        self.displayed_month = displayed_month
        self.on_displayed_month_changed = on_displayed_month_changed
        self.initial_calendar_mode = initial_calendar_mode
        self.header = header
        self.months = months
        self.surface = surface
        self.outside_days_visible = outside_days_visible
        self.six_week_months_enforced = six_week_months_enforced
        self.starting_day_of_week = starting_day_of_week
        self.selectable_day_predicate = selectable_day_predicate
        self.disabled_dates = disabled_dates
        self.disabled_weekdays = disabled_weekdays
        self.markers = markers
        self.event_loader = event_loader
        self.markers_max_count = markers_max_count
        self.day_builder = day_builder
        self.day_style = day_style
        self.year_style = year_style
        self.locale = locale
        self.time_zone = time_zone
        self.label = label
        self.style = style

    # The calendar's own reporting, over this class's keywords.
    _DEFAULTS = CalendarDatePicker._DEFAULTS
    _PROPS = tuple("range_length" if k == "multiple" else k
                   for k in CalendarDatePicker._PROPS)
    props = CalendarDatePicker.props


class YearPicker(BaseWidget):
    """A grid of years to pick one from — Flutter's ``YearPicker``, drawn
    as the design's year view (calendar.html), three to a row.

    ``value`` is the picked year, a whole number (``2025``), or ``None``;
    ``on_change`` receives the year pressed. Pressing the picked year again
    does nothing::

        YearPicker(value=self.year, on_change=self.set_year,
                   first_date="1950-01-01", last_date="2030-12-31")

    Every year from ``first_date``'s to ``last_date``'s is listed (1900 to
    2100 by default), in a box that scrolls, with the picked year centred
    when it opens; at most 300 are built at a time, around the one focused,
    and they move as the keys or a scroll reach their edge, so a mouse or
    a finger reaches every year too. The year of ``current_date`` (default:
    today) is marked for a screen reader. Inside a ``Field`` the picker is
    the field's value, the year as ``on_change`` got it.

    **Looks.** ``surface="flat"`` drops the card, ``year_style`` is a
    ``style=`` dict for every year (state keys included: ``":hover"``,
    ``":selected"``, ``":current"`` for this year), and ``style=`` takes the
    ``--calendar-*`` tokens. Year labels follow ``locale``; ``label`` names
    the picker (its role=group) for a screen reader, and the grid of years
    inside it is always "Choose year" (the ``calendar.choose_year`` token).
    """

    widget_type = "YearPicker"

    def __init__(
        self,
        *,
        value: int | Binding | None = None,
        on_change: Callable | None = None,
        first_date: str | Binding | None = None,
        last_date: str | Binding | None = None,
        current_date: str | Binding | None = None,
        surface: Literal["card", "flat"] | Binding = "card",
        year_style: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        locale: str | Binding | None = None,
        label: str | Binding | None = None,
        disabled: bool | Binding = False,
        style: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        key: str | None = None,
        **kwargs: Any,
    ):
        super().__init__(key=key, disabled=disabled, **kwargs)
        self.value = value
        self.on_change = on_change
        self.first_date = first_date
        self.last_date = last_date
        self.current_date = current_date
        self.surface = surface
        self.year_style = year_style
        self.locale = locale
        self.label = label
        self.style = style

    # The calendar's own reporting, over this class's keywords and default.
    _DEFAULTS = {"surface": "card"}
    _PROPS = ("value", "first_date", "last_date", "current_date", "surface",
              "year_style", "locale", "label", "style")
    props = CalendarDatePicker.props


class TimePicker(BaseWidget):
    """A time of day picked on columns that scroll — the design's Time
    Picker (calendar.html), a wheel like Cupertino's, with its parameters.

    ``value`` is ``"14:30"``, or ``"14:30:15"`` with ``seconds=True``,
    always in 24 hours whatever the wheel shows, or ``None``; ``on_change``
    receives the time once the wheel comes to rest, never on every scroll::

        TimePicker(value=self.at, on_change=self.set_at)
        TimePicker(value=self.at, on_change=self.set_at,
                   minute_interval=15, first_time="09:00",
                   last_time="17:30")

    There is a column for the hours, the minutes, the seconds with
    ``seconds=True``, and AM/PM on a 12-hour clock, in the order the
    locale writes a time (``ko`` puts AM/PM first). ``use_24h_format``
    decides between 12 and 24 hours; ``None``, the default, follows the
    locale. Hour 00 is 12 AM and hour 12 is 12 PM.

    ``minute_interval`` steps the minutes (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 10, 12, 15,
    20 or 30); a value between steps (``"10:07"`` at 5) is shown as it is,
    on a cell of its own, and the next move lands back on a step.
    ``first_time`` and ``last_time`` bound it, both included: a cell
    outside them is shown disabled, the keys skip it, and a wheel left on
    one comes to rest on the nearest time inside them.

    A scroll, a flick, a press on a cell or a key moves the wheel without
    re-rendering the page; once it settles, ``on_change`` is called once,
    with every column's centred cell. With ``value=None`` the wheel rests
    on ``first_time`` (midnight by default) with no cell picked. Inside a
    ``Field`` the picker is the field's value: validators read the time,
    or ``""`` with none, so ``required`` refuses an empty one.

    **Looks.** The design's 132px card for three columns, 40px more for
    each other one. ``style=`` takes the ``--time-picker-*`` tokens, and
    the picked row and the ring around it follow ``--calendar-range-bg``
    and ``--calendar-day-bg-selected``, so a calendar and its wheel
    retint together. ``label`` names the wheel for a screen reader
    (default "Time").
    """

    widget_type = "TimePicker"

    def __init__(
        self,
        *,
        value: str | Binding | None = None,
        on_change: Callable | None = None,
        seconds: bool | Binding = False,
        use_24h_format: bool | Binding | None = None,
        minute_interval: int | Binding = 1,
        first_time: str | Binding | None = None,
        last_time: str | Binding | None = None,
        locale: str | Binding | None = None,
        label: str | Binding | None = None,
        disabled: bool | Binding = False,
        style: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        key: str | None = None,
        **kwargs: Any,
    ):
        super().__init__(key=key, disabled=disabled, **kwargs)
        self.value = value
        self.on_change = on_change
        self.seconds = seconds
        self.use_24h_format = use_24h_format
        self.minute_interval = minute_interval
        self.first_time = first_time
        self.last_time = last_time
        self.locale = locale
        self.label = label
        self.style = style

    # The calendar's own reporting, over this class's keywords and defaults.
    _DEFAULTS = {"seconds": False, "minute_interval": 1}
    _PROPS = ("value", "seconds", "use_24h_format", "minute_interval",
              "first_time", "last_time", "locale", "label", "style")
    props = CalendarDatePicker.props


#: ``kind=``: what a typed field holds — a date, a time of day, or both.
DATE_SEGMENTS_KINDS = ("date", "time", "datetime")

#: ``appearance=``: the design's field (input.css), or the pill a time row
#: draws beside its label (calendar.css).
DATE_SEGMENTS_APPEARANCES = ("field", "pill")


class DateSegments(BaseWidget):
    """A date or a time typed a part at a time — the design's Date & Time
    field (inputs.html), each part its own spinbutton, as a native
    ``datetime-local`` has them.

    ``kind`` is ``"date"`` (month, day, year), ``"time"`` (hour, minute,
    AM/PM, and seconds with ``seconds=True``) or ``"datetime"`` (both), a
    literal, since it decides what the value is. ``value`` is an ISO string
    of that kind — ``"2025-12-16"``, ``"14:30"``, ``"2025-12-16T14:30"`` —
    or ``None``; ``on_change`` receives one once every part is filled and
    it names a real date (a time) inside the bounds, and ``None`` once if
    the field had a value and stops naming one. What is typed in between is
    the field's own, kept through the page's re-renders::

        DateSegments(value=self.due, on_change=self.set_due)
        DateSegments(kind="time", value=self.at, on_change=self.set_at,
                     seconds=True, show_zone=True)

    The parts come in the order the locale writes a date (``en-US``
    month/day/year, ``en-GB`` day/month/year, ``ja-JP`` year/month/day),
    12 or 24 hours by ``use_24h_format`` (``None`` follows the locale). A
    part takes digits and moves on by itself once no further digit could
    fit (a 2 in the month, a 4 in the day, four digits of a year); ↑ ↓ step
    it, wrapping round, the minutes by ``minute_interval``; ← → move
    between the parts; Backspace clears one; ``a`` or ``p`` sets AM/PM; a
    paste of a whole ISO value of the kind fills every part.

    ``first_date``/``last_date``, ``disabled_weekdays``, ``disabled_dates``
    and ``selectable_day_predicate`` (a date's), and ``first_time``/
    ``last_time`` (a time's), rule values out as a calendar's and a wheel's
    do: a whole value they refuse is not handed on, and the field says
    ``error_invalid_text`` ("Choose a date in range"); one left half typed
    says ``error_format_text`` ("Enter a complete date"), once the person
    has left it. Inside an ``Input`` those words are its message; inside a
    ``Field`` they are the field's error, and the form does not submit.
    ``show_zone=True`` adds the zone's short name for the value's date
    (``time_zone``, else the viewer's): EST in December, EDT in July.

    ``appearance="pill"`` is calendar.html's time pill. Written inside
    ``Input(...)`` it fuses into the Input's box, which names it with its
    label: ``Input(Icon("calendar"), DateSegments(…), label="Due")``.
    A press on the month or the day opens a calendar beneath the field (the
    year part opens it on its years), a time part or a time field's clock
    opens the wheel, and Alt+↓ opens either with focus inside it;
    ``picker=False`` leaves them out. ``current_date`` and
    ``starting_day_of_week`` are that calendar's today and first day of the
    week. ``label`` names the field for a screen reader when it stands
    alone.
    """

    widget_type = "DateSegments"

    def __init__(
        self,
        *,
        kind: Literal["date", "time", "datetime"] = "date",
        value: str | Binding | None = None,
        on_change: Callable | None = None,
        appearance: Literal["field", "pill"] | Binding = "field",
        picker: bool | Binding = True,
        seconds: bool | Binding = False,
        use_24h_format: bool | Binding | None = None,
        minute_interval: int | Binding = 1,
        show_zone: bool | Binding = False,
        time_zone: str | Binding | None = None,
        locale: str | Binding | None = None,
        first_date: str | Binding | None = None,
        last_date: str | Binding | None = None,
        current_date: str | Binding | None = None,
        selectable_day_predicate: Callable | None = None,
        disabled_dates: list | Binding | None = None,
        disabled_weekdays: list | Binding | None = None,
        starting_day_of_week: int | Binding | None = None,
        first_time: str | Binding | None = None,
        last_time: str | Binding | None = None,
        error_format_text: str | Binding | None = None,
        error_invalid_text: str | Binding | None = None,
        label: str | Binding | None = None,
        disabled: bool | Binding = False,
        style: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        key: str | None = None,
        **kwargs: Any,
    ):
        super().__init__(key=key, disabled=disabled, **kwargs)
        self.kind = kind
        self.value = value
        self.on_change = on_change
        self.appearance = appearance
        self.picker = picker
        self.seconds = seconds
        self.use_24h_format = use_24h_format
        self.minute_interval = minute_interval
        self.show_zone = show_zone
        self.time_zone = time_zone
        self.locale = locale
        self.first_date = first_date
        self.last_date = last_date
        self.current_date = current_date
        self.selectable_day_predicate = selectable_day_predicate
        self.disabled_dates = disabled_dates
        self.disabled_weekdays = disabled_weekdays
        self.starting_day_of_week = starting_day_of_week
        self.first_time = first_time
        self.last_time = last_time
        self.error_format_text = error_format_text
        self.error_invalid_text = error_invalid_text
        self.label = label
        self.style = style

    # The calendar's own reporting, over this class's keywords and defaults.
    _DEFAULTS = {"kind": "date", "appearance": "field", "picker": True,
                 "seconds": False, "minute_interval": 1, "show_zone": False}
    _PROPS = ("kind", "value", "appearance", "picker", "seconds",
              "use_24h_format", "minute_interval", "show_zone", "time_zone",
              "locale", "first_date", "last_date", "current_date",
              "disabled_dates", "disabled_weekdays", "starting_day_of_week",
              "first_time", "last_time", "error_format_text",
              "error_invalid_text", "label", "style")
    props = CalendarDatePicker.props


#: ``direction=``: the design's picker panel (a column: quick ranges, the
#: month, its time rows) or its preset picker (a row: the list beside the
#: month).
CALENDAR_CARD_DIRECTIONS = ("column", "row")


class CalendarCard(BaseWidget):
    """The card a composed date picker sits in — the design's picker panel
    (calendar.html's ``.calendar-panel``) and preset picker
    (``.calendar-preset-picker``): the calendar's own card chrome around
    whatever it holds, so a calendar inside it is drawn flat
    (``surface="flat"``)::

        CalendarCard(
            ButtonGroup(Button("Today", on_click=self.today),
                        Button("Week", on_click=self.week),
                        variant="tertiary-gray", rounded=True),
            CalendarDatePicker(value=self.day, on_change=self.set_day,
                               surface="flat"),
        )

    ``direction="column"`` (the default) stacks its children 16px apart,
    and stretches a ``ButtonGroup`` in it across the card, its buttons
    sharing the width, as the design's quick-range row is drawn;
    ``direction="row"`` lays them side by side 20px apart. ``gap``
    replaces either, in pixels. The card hugs what it holds wherever it
    is put, and its chrome is the calendar's: the ``--calendar-card-bg``,
    ``--calendar-card-border``, ``--calendar-card-shadow``,
    ``--calendar-radius`` and ``--calendar-pad`` tokens, set on it with
    ``style=`` or on any box around it. It also caps the rows a panel
    builds from the calendar library at one month's width
    (``--calendar-row-max``, 300px inside the card).

    A field's time wheel opened from inside the card dims the whole card
    behind it, as the design's popover does: the card is the host the
    wheel looks for. ``direction`` is a literal; ``gap`` may come from
    state.
    """

    widget_type = "CalendarCard"

    def __init__(
        self,
        *children: WidgetChild,
        direction: Literal["column", "row"] = "column",
        gap: int | Binding | None = None,
        style: dict[str, Any] | None = None,
        key: str | None = None,
        **kwargs: Any,
    ):
        super().__init__(key=key, **kwargs)
        self.content = list(children)
        self.direction = direction
        self.gap = gap
        self.style = style

    def children(self) -> list[Any]:
        return list(self.content)

    def props(self) -> dict[str, Any]:
        p: dict[str, Any] = {}
        if self.direction != "column":
            p["direction"] = self.direction
        for name in ("gap", "style"):
            if getattr(self, name) is not None:
                p[name] = getattr(self, name)
        return p
